    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If the protonation is increasing the electrostatic repulsion, would that eventually lead to coalescence of the droplets? Also, how much does the viscosity of the continuous phase play into preventing that separation in a low-fat system where there's less oil to provide structure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, increased electrostatic repulsion due to protonated lecithin headgroups can ultimately lead to droplet coalescence. As the pH decreases, the lecithin headgroups become more protonated, generating more electrostatic stress across the interface. This can lead to the formation of a \"skin\" of repulsion around the droplets, destabilizing the oil droplets and causing them to coalesce or merge.\n\nThe viscosity of the continuous phase also plays a crucial role in stabilizing the emulsion. In low-fat systems, the continuous phase (usually a liquid, such as water or an oil-free base) provides the primary structural support. A higher viscosity of the continuous phase can help to prevent droplet coalescence by increasing the energy required for droplet motion and subsequent coalescence. However, in low-fat systems, achieving the optimal viscosity can be a significant challenge. Using emulsifiers with inherent thickening properties or thickeners that can be added to the system may be necessary to stabilize the emulsion.",
      "source": "target",
