    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, so if we can't rely on the oil volume for viscosity, we're looking at hydrocolloids or gums to stabilize the continuous phase. I'm thinking about using Xanthan or a modified starch. From a purely kinetic standpoint, if the droplets are under that \"electrostatic stress\" you mentioned, how does the choice of stabilizer affect the rate of collision between droplets? I'm trying to figure out if we can kinetically trap them before those repulsive forces cause a breakdown.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
